@@ -72,6 +72,8 @@ While a turn waits, the warm ticks prepare most of the swap (`WorldEpoch._prep`:
 
 **Kept after the swap (2026-10-30).** The swap does not drop the ring job and the preparation it used: it retires them (`WorldEpoch._retire`), keyed by the world it turned away from, for `KEEP_TICKS` (21, NetSession's `maxAhead` 20 and one) sim ticks; `tick()` sweeps what has aged out, and `SimCore.dispose` releases a disposed sim's (`WorldEpoch.release`, which also drops every world the sim made entries for; `NetSnapshot.restore` disposes with `keepWorld`, since the restored state plays on). A rollback that restores a state of that world and runs the swap again finds them: the swap tick is the swap and not a ring read plus a preparation (`rerun_cost.gd`: about 60 ms before (90 to 107 on a loaded machine) and about 2 ms after, on a Half ring, with the same state hash). The kept preparation is safe to use twice because the swap binds its structures `fresh` (the prepared buildings and trees stay pristine) and the kept buildings' districts are generation records the restore puts back (packed-structures.md).
 
+**Kept after a cancel or a new pick (2026-10-30; Netcode's find).** `cancel` and `choose` used to drop the world's ring jobs and preparation at once, so a rollback to before a cancel (the restored state counts the ring as read) read the whole ring on the rollback frame: up to 274 ms on gen 2 Whole, 130 on Half. They are now kept as the swap's are: the live jobs and preparation belong to one pick (its tick and pivot column, `_livePk`); the next call with another pick shelves them under their own pick (`_sync`, `_shelf`), a rollback into the first pick takes them back, and everything ages out `KEEP_TICKS` after it was last used (`_sweep`). It also closes a latent fault: the jobs were keyed by world and bearing only, so a rollback into pick A after pick B (same bearing, another pivot) would have used B's ring for A; the pick is now part of what they belong to. `WorldEpoch.catchupCols` (a process counter, not state) counts columns a job had to read at once to catch up; it stays 0 across a rollback (`epoch_check`, three rollback cases with the old behaviour as the negative control). No state, hash or data changes.
+
 ## What is not here
 
 - **Steering** (the side from the brawl's drag and the last launch, the pick by combat alchemy's score) is Encounter's; World gives the candidates and applies the pick. The check stands in for Encounter with a scripted ask.
